Kubernetes Probes vs Load Balancer Health Checks

A service can appear healthy inside Kubernetes but still be unavailable to users through the load balancer.

That’s because Kubernetes probes and load balancer health checks serve different purposes:
- Liveness Probe
Checks whether a container should be restarted when it becomes unhealthy.
- Readiness Probe
Determines whether a pod is ready to receive traffic from a Kubernetes Service.
- Startup Probe
Gives slow-starting applications time to initialize before liveness and readiness checks begin.
- Load Balancer Health Check
Checks whether a backend endpoint is reachable and capable of serving external traffic.

Common Production Issue
Your readiness probe may return 200 OK, but the load balancer could still fail because of:
- Incorrect health-check paths or ports
- Firewall or security-group restrictions
- Misconfigured target groups
- Network policies blocking traffic
- Health checks reaching the wrong endpoint
